## Ordered summary of validations and side-effects for POST /api/owners

1. `@PreAuthorize` security check — caller must have `ROLE_OWNER_ADMIN`.
2. `@Valid` on the request body (interface + `@Validated`) runs Bean Validation on `OwnerFieldsDto`: `@NotNull` on all 5 fields, `@Size` on all 5, `@Pattern` on firstName/lastName/telephone. Failure → 400 (handled by `ExceptionControllerAdvice`).
3. Controller `validateRequiredFields` — rejects null/blank (whitespace-only) firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone; throws `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` → 400 with `errors` list.
4. `ownerMapper.toOwner(dto)` — builds `Owner` with `id` ignored (null) and `pets` ignored.
5. `clinicService.saveOwner(owner)` — `@Transactional`, delegates to `ownerRepository.save`.
6. `ownerRepository.save` — Spring Data default persist (insert, IDENTITY-generated id); no custom logic, no lifecycle callbacks, no events.
7. Controller maps saved `Owner` back to `OwnerDto`, sets `Location: /api/owners/{id}`, returns 201 Created.

No JPA entity listeners, no `@PrePersist`/lifecycle callbacks, no application events, and no custom `ConstraintValidator`s are involved anywhere in this path.
